# The dashboard's whole configuration surface. Copy to `.env.local`.
#
# Everything here ends up readable in the built bundle, and that is fine —
# the dashboard holds no secrets and has no direct database access. The
# service-role key must NEVER appear in this file or any other under this
# directory; it lives in Supabase's function secrets and nowhere else.
#
# Leave both unset to run against the in-browser mock: `MockClient` for the
# API and a simulated sign-in that says so on the login screen. Set both and
# the dashboard points at the real backend — real Supabase Auth sessions and
# (once `HttpClient` lands) the real v1 API. They move together; a real
# session in front of a browser-side ledger is a lie about which one you are
# looking at.

# VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://mwnbjjlilqrwdmwutbxr.supabase.co

# The anon key. Not a credential: it identifies the project to the auth server
# and authorises nothing on its own. Every real check is the session JWT, or
# the API key a client's server holds.
# VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Point the Intelligence screens at the real Edge Functions — the first slice
# of `HttpClient`. Needs both variables above set as well.
#
# Separate from them on purpose. Real sessions and a real ledger move together;
# real *drafts* over mock deals do not work at all, because the model would be
# asked about a dispute that exists in this browser and in no database. Setting
# this is a claim that the backend holds your tenant's data, and only you know
# whether that is true. Leave it unset and the AI screens run on the mock, which
# is a deterministic stand-in with no network and no key.
# VITE_PAYHOLD_AI_LIVE=1
